  <h2 style="margin-top:44px">Why dimensions matter</h2>
  <p>Dimensions are the grammar of architecture. A plan can look beautiful and still fail because a corridor is too tight to pass two people, a stair is too steep to climb comfortably, or a door swings into the toilet. These numbers come from the human body — how far we reach, how much room we need to turn, sit or rest — so getting them right is what makes a space quietly <em>work</em>. Get them wrong and no amount of styling saves it.</p>
  <p>They’re also the fastest way to look like you know what you’re doing in a crit. Drawing a stair at a believable 175&nbsp;mm rise, or a parking bay at 2.4&nbsp;×&nbsp;4.8&nbsp;m, signals that your scheme could actually be built — not just rendered.</p>

  <h3>How to use these pages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>As a lookup.</strong> Grab the number you need mid-project — riser height, door width, turning circle — without digging through a code book.</li>
    <li><strong>To understand the reasoning.</strong> Each page explains where the figure comes from, so you can adapt it sensibly instead of memorising it.</li>
    <li><strong>Start from the person.</strong> Almost every standard traces back to <a href="/standards/human-scale-anthropometrics/">human scale and anthropometrics</a> — learn those first and the rest follows.</li>
    <li><strong>Then confirm the code.</strong> These are trustworthy general figures; the legal minimums shift by country, occupancy and building type — always check the <a href="/building-codes/">code that applies to your project</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:24px">
